#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133576 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40bbc65e-5ee4-5ce6-aaf7-d0f9968c0c51" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"}] :question/id #uuid "792b0c63-15eb-5b0e-b5d6-01ae5b68eeca" :question/math-academy-id "e-7134" :question/problem "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ and $y=1-x$ from $y=0$ to $y=1$, as shown below, then what is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x^2y+xy^2) \\, \\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2152/Source/Images/77c8f86821d718dbfe450f51148a59e3.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x-2}\n$$\n\nand $y=1-x$ in terms of $y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y = \\sqrt{x - 2} & ⟹x = y^{2} + 2 \\\\ y = 1 - x & ⟹x = 1 - y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2152/Source/Images/6bcc7bf82e91ec4c15377d04f25681af.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\bigg\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad 1-y \\leq x \\leq y^2+2 \\bigg\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)(x^{2}y + xy^{2})dA &= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{1 - y}^{y^{2} + 2}(x^{2}y + xy^{2})dxdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133577 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ccbe70d-24f4-5e9c-8536-9ccab99f571b" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"}] :question/id #uuid "e005ebb9-6c14-529e-be8f-9b837e45eef1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7135" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=3-2x$ below the line $y=3$. What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} f\\left({x,y} \\right) \\, \\mathrm{d}A\\:$ expressed as a repeated integral?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2152/Source/Images/1c78772e8e145e3e09dbc2d763e7f1cb.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=x^2$ and $y=3-2x$ in terms of $y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y = x^{2} & ⟹x = \\sqrt{y} \\\\ y = 3 - 2x & ⟹x = \\frac{3 - y}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y$:\n$\\sqrt{y}|= \\frac{3 - y}{2}; 4y|= (3 - y)^{2}; 4y|= 9 - 6y + y^{2}; y^{2} - 10y + 9|= 0; (y - 1)(y - 9)|= 0$So, since our region lies below the line $y=3$, the $y$-limits are\n$1 \\le y \\le 3$.\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2152/Source/Images/d3c6291cf3fb20c734e14cf5a74027de.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 3, \\quad \\dfrac{3-y}{2} \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)f(x, y)dA &= ∫_{1}^{3}∫_{(3 - y)/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x, y)dxdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133578 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fdce40e5-8ffe-52cb-8a8e-ac8eaea982f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/id #uuid "ebd5bc16-2df2-5014-a1a2-bdebf2afa195" :question/math-academy-id "e-7136" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{\\sqrt{\\pi}}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\int_0^{y^2} \\frac{\\sin y^2}{y} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\,\\mathrm{d}y$." :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}}∫_{0}^{y^{2}}\\frac{\\sin y^{2}}{y}dxdy &= ∫_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}}\\frac{\\sin y^{2}}{y}[∫_{0}^{y^{2}}dx]dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}}\\frac{\\sin y^{2}}{y}[x]_{0}^{y^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}}\\frac{\\sin y^{2}}{y}(y^{2} - 0)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}}y\\sin y^{2}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}}y\\sin y^{2}dy &= [- \\frac{1}{2}\\cos y^{2}]_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}(\\cos 2π - \\cos π) \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133579 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f7718f-e014-5232-acb1-6773ab79f61d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/id #uuid "ad2ef01a-d49d-5b17-9066-d5c3970caf31" :question/math-academy-id "e-7137" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\:$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $x^2+y^2=4$ and $x=0$, and above the line $y=-1$, as shown in the picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2152/Source/Images/d3efc009081220a488deb11b6d536fce.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since\n\n$$\nx \\ge 0\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following equations in terms of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} + y^{2} = 4 & ⟹x = \\sqrt{4 - y^{2}} \\\\ x = 0 & ⟹x = 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y$:\n$\\sqrt{4 - y^{2}}|= 0; 4 - y^{2}|= 0; y^{2}|= 4; y|= ± 2$.\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=-1$, the $y$-limits are\n$-1 \\le y \\le 2$.\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2152/Source/Images/8b3b3ffed1e4aeb6dd4c9e503c34687d.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{4-y^2} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A = \\int_{-1}^2 \\int_0^{\\sqrt{4-y^2}}\\, xy^2\\, \\mathrm dx\\, \\mathrm{d} y\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1}^{2}∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{4 - y^{2}}}xy^{2}dxdy &= ∫_{-1}^{2}y^{2}[∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{4 - y^{2}}}xdx]dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{2}y^{2}[\\frac{x^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{\\sqrt{4 - y^{2}}}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{2}\\frac{y^{2}}{2}(4 - y^{2} - 0)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{2}(2y^{2} - \\frac{y^{4}}{2})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1}^{2}(2y^{2} - \\frac{y^{4}}{2})dy &= [\\frac{2}{3}y^{3} - \\frac{y^{5}}{10}]_{-1}^{2} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{16}{3} - \\frac{32}{10}) - (-\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{1}{10}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{27}{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40bbc65e-5ee4-5ce6-aaf7-d0f9968c0c51" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "894b2a6e-4ff7-5edc-8a67-ca3f02cfe95a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{y/2}∫_{-y}^{4}(x^{2} + y^{2})dxdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131775} {:answer/id #uuid "a604efcf-9afb-5962-afe3-a42ce165d446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-y}^{y/2}∫_{0}^{4}(x^{2} + y^{2})dxdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131776} {:answer/id #uuid "317ca957-9dfd-546a-85c3-d566372240ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{4}∫_{-y}^{y/2}(x^{2} + y^{2})dxdy" :db/id #edb